1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
In this lecture, we're going to get a bit more advanced into ISA topics.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Let's discuss how the MIPS assembly instructions are represented in the machine.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,000
All instructions are represented in a single word.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This shows how an add instruction is encoded into bit fields

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
as shown in the MIPS green card under the basic instruction format header.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
As you can see, some fields specify the operand registers

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
and others specify the app code.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
The app code found in the most significant six bits

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
specify which operation and instruction format.

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Register operations are called R-format,

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
intermediate are called I-format,

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
and jump are called J-format.

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Depending on the format, the operand bits are interpreted differently.

14
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
R-format is used for most ALU operations.

15
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,000
RS and RT specify the source operand registers,

16
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
and RD specifies the destination.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
The shift amount is only used for shift instructions,

18
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
and function can provide another encoding for more differentiation.

19
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
For example, an add instruction is opcode 0 with a function hex20,

20
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:26,000
while an add unsigned is opcode 1 with a function hex21.

21
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The next I-format, which is used for instructions

22
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,000
that take a 16-bit intermediate value in the least significant bits.

23
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
An intermediate value is used as a constant

24
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,000
because it's baked into the program as opposed to a variable in a register.

25
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
This shows how a load word instruction is encoded.

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:51,000
In this specific case, load word is going to use register2 as a base address,

27
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:55,000
add an offset of 72 to it to make the pointer,

28
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,000
and load the data stored at that address.

29
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,000
This intermediate value is signed,

30
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
meaning the pointer can be greater than or less than the base address.

31
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Another variant of I-format is used for any ALU operations with intermediates also.

32
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
In this case, addI will add 100 to the contents of register2

33
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
and store it in register1.

34
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
What if we need an intermediate that's larger than 16 bits?

35
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Here's a 20-bit constant that we'll need to load into registers.

36
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,000
We can use the loadUpperImmediate instruction to take the 12 from bit 16 and above

37
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
of my desired constant and store it in the upper 16 bits of the target register.

38
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Now we can use logical or immediate to set the lower bits and we're done.

39
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Branches also use I-format.

40
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Branches immediate are word-sized offsets based on the next instruction address

41
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
since there's never a need to branch to the next address

42
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
because that's what happens by default.

43
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Internally, the word offset is shifted left2 or multiplied by 4 to get the byte address,

44
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:21,000
so in a way, we've hidden the lowest two bits and gotten more branch range in return.

45
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Last up is J-format for jump instructions.

46
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,000
There are only two fields, the opcode and a 26-bit offset.

47
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Like branch, we get the two hidden bits at the bottom for free,

48
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
so we get a much larger 28 bits of word-aligned byte addressing available

49
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,000
than the 18 bits we get in branch.

50
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:51,000
This is the summary of instruction formats, which is also found in the MIPS green card.

51
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,000
The stack is used to hold anything you need including parameters, return values, and return address.

52
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
The stack grows from higher to lower addresses.

53
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:09,000
SP is a label for register 29 and it always points to the top or most recent word.

54
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Pushing a value to the stack involves first decrementing the stack pointer by four bytes,

55
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
or one word, and then storing a value to that address.

56
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Popping a word happens in reverse.

57
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Load from the stack pointer and then add four.

58
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
When calling functions, the application binary interface, or ABI,

59
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
is the convention used to define how functions must operate.

60
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Unlike C, which is a scope that defines which variables exist,

61
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:49,000
functions executing on a MIPS processor share the same 32 registers.

62
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:56,000
As you'd expect, the call conventions are documented in the MIPS green card.

63
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:02,000
If a caller needs to save the values in any registers that are not guaranteed to be preserved across a function call,

64
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
it's their responsibility to save them to the stack.

65
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Temporary registers, T0 through T7, are one kind of register that is not guaranteed to be saved.

66
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Now, the callee may not use them, but the ABI says they're free to overwrite them.

67
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Please take a look at the list in the MIPS green card to see which registers are callee saved

68
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:34,000
and which are caller saved and which are callee saved.

69
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Before calling a function, the caller must push any caller saved registers to the stack before they call the function,

70
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
and then they must pop them when the function returns.

71
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Likewise, the callee must save any registers it is going to use that are supposed to be preserved across a function call.

72
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:01,000
The saved temporary registers, S0 through S7, are eight such registers.

73
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
The others are the pointers and return address.

74
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Let's consider the snippet of code that implements the swap function in the upper right.

75
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:17,000
To start, the function arguments are put into A0 and A1.

76
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Since K is a word index, it must be multiplied by 4, which is the same as shifted left 2.

77
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Adding the word index to the base address enables us to load V at offset K.

78
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:43,000
T0 is a callee saved register, so the function is free to overwrite it with the value loaded from memory.

79
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Now we load the next word, V, at offset K plus 1.

80
00:06:50,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Now that we have V sub K and V sub K plus 1 in registers, we just have to store them out swapped relative to how we loaded them.

81
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:05,000
And finally, jump to the return address.

82
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:10,000
What can go wrong if we use S1 instead of T1?

83
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:16,000
Well, let's say the caller had a crucial value in S1 already.

84
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:24,000
If it called swap and swap overwrote it, now the next add instruction won't do what the programmer thought it should.

85
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Remember that S1 is a callee saved register.

86
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:42,000
If we needed to use S1 inside of our swap function, we first need to push it to the stack in the beginning of our function and pop it before we jump to the return address.

87
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Now let's cover addressing modes and MIPS.

88
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
The first is register addressing.

89
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:53,000
The value in the register field decodes to the register that's read.

90
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:58,000
Base addressing, also known as offset or displacement addressing.

91
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:03,000
The register contains the base address and an immediate value contains an offset.

92
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:08,000
These two values are summed to get the effective address.

93
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Immediate addressing is used in I-format instructions.

94
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
PC relative addressing is used for branch instructions.

95
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:31,000
This is similar to branch or displacement, but now the base comes from PC plus four and is used to load the next instruction.

96
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Some ISAs have addressing modes that are not found in MIPS.

97
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
Let's take a look at a few of them.

98
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:46,000
Indexed, where two registers are added to form an address as opposed to one register and an immediate offset like MIPS.

99
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Then there's indirect addressing, where a register contains a pointer and a location in memory that contains another pointer to the value.

100
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Finally, auto increment and auto update, which implicitly adds plus one or plus four to the register after using it to compute a memory offset.

101
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,000
Auto update is useful with arrays.

102
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:16,000
In this case, the immediate offset 24 is automatically added to register two.

103
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Now, if every block of data is 24 bytes, this function skips to the beginning of the next block.

104
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:29,000
This example demonstrates how you would convert a loop to an auto update.

105
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
The add immediate becomes implicit in the instruction.

106
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:41,000
You do need to be careful as the first address that is loaded is s one plus four instead of s one plus zero in the original code.

107
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:49,000
To avoid this issue, you must subtract four from the base address before entering the loop.

108
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:53,000
Another major topic in ISAs is endian-ness.

109
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:57,000
It specifies how bytes within a word are addressed.

110
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:03,000
With big endian, the least significant byte is put at the highest address.

111
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:09,000
For 32-bit architectures, the last two bits will always be one one.

112
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
IBM and Spark both use big endian.

113
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:18,000
Little endian places the least significant byte at the lowest address and it will end in zero zero.

114
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:22,000
x86 and our favorite MIPS ISA use little endian.

115
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:29,000
To improve performance, some architectures have added a configuration to support both.

116
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:40,000
Endian-ness causes headaches for architects with pointer arithmetic and transferring data types between machines.

117
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
Let's revisit our add example from before.

118
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:53,000
If this were packed to little endian, the least significant byte would be put at address offset zero zero and the most significant byte would be put at address offset one one.

119
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Big endian is simply the reverse.

120
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:02,000
The little in little endian refers to the least significant byte at address zero,

121
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:09,000
while big endian is motivated for byte serial transfers where the upper bits are sent first.

122
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:14,000
Regardless of endian-ness, when displaying four bytes like this instruction,

123
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:24,000
bit 31 will always be most significant on the left and zero will always be least significant and on the right.

124
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:30,000
Let's end this lecture by looking at an exercise to check your understanding of more advanced topics.

125
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:42,000
Grab your MIPS green card again and pause the video so you can work this out.

126
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
Let's start with a simple one.

127
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:49,000
We know that I needs to be converted to word offset for an integer array,

128
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:54,000
so shifting left two is the same as multiplying by four.

129
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
Next we see that I times four is added to register S zero,

130
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:11,000
so S zero must be used as the base address of the integer array A and hex 100 is 256 in decimal.

131
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:17,000
Next we see the value we load from memory is stored in T three, which is then added to something.

132
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:27,000
This must be the sum variable, so T three must be added to the current value of the sum variable T zero.

133
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
Finally, all that's left is the loop condition.

134
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Since N is 64, S one must hold the offset to the first address past the end of the array.

135
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:44,000
We need to check that I is less than N using the set less than instruction.

136
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:48,000
Now let's change the do while loop to a for loop.

137
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Is our assembly code still valid?

138
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:59,000
Please pause the video and ponder.

139
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:02,000
Consider when the conditionals are observed.

140
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,000
In a do while, the condition is checked at the end of the loop.

141
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:11,000
While in a for loop, it's checked in the beginning of each iteration.

142
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
Here's a new listing with some blanks.

143
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:25,000
Please pause and consider and work it out.

144
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:34,000
At the top of the loop, we set T four to a one if T one is still less than S one, the end variable.

145
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:38,000
Then we must branch to the end of the loop if T four is not set.

146
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:48,000
At the bottom, we have an unconditional loop back to the top.

147
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:51,000
Let's return to instruction optimization.

148
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
This is the common one that compilers will use.

149
00:13:54,000 --> 00:14:06,000
The observation is that T one is an integer index offset, which serves no other purpose than to go from zero to 63.

150
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:14,000
We can instead replace all occurrences of T one with S zero, the register that initially holds the base address of the array.

151
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,000
It can be used as the iterator itself.

152
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:23,000
As the integer iterator, it must be updated by four at the bottom of the loop.

153
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:36,000
And now S one can hold the end byte address of the entire array instead of the last integer offset number.

154
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:42,000
With that, we've removed three instructions from the program.

